#: ../../../gpu-safety/bounds-checks.md:28
msgid "What a bounds check costs"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../gpu-safety/bounds-checks.md:30
msgid "A dot product between a row of A and a column of B looks harmless:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../gpu-safety/bounds-checks.md:32
msgid ""
"let mut sum = 0.0f32;\n"
"let mut i = 0;\n"
"while i < k {\n"
"    sum += a[row * k + i] * b[i * n + col];\n"
"    i += 1;\n"
"}\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../gpu-safety/bounds-checks.md:41
msgid "Count what each iteration actually executes:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../gpu-safety/bounds-checks.md:43
msgid ""
"i < k              the loop's own exit test        (necessary)\n"
"row*k + i < a.len  bounds check, branch to trap    (overhead)\n"
"load a\n"
"i*n + col < b.len  bounds check, branch to trap    (overhead)\n"
"load b\n"
"fma\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../gpu-safety/bounds-checks.md:52
msgid ""
"Two of the three compares are overhead, and they also split the loop body "
"into multiple basic blocks, which blocks unrolling. The compiler cannot "
"remove them on its own: it sees `a.len()` as an opaque number and has no way "
"to know that `row * k + i` stays below it. *You* know it -- because you "
"sized the buffers on the host -- but that fact lives in a different program. "
"The rest of this chapter is about handing that fact to the compiler."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../gpu-safety/bounds-checks.md:61
msgid "Read views: check a whole strip once"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../gpu-safety/bounds-checks.md:63
msgid ""
"A matrix lives in GPU memory as one long array, row after row. The **row "
"width** (called `stride` in the API) is how many elements one row occupies. "
"Element `(row, col)` lives at flat index `row * stride + col`:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../gpu-safety/bounds-checks.md:67
msgid ""
" 3 x 4 matrix, stride = 4:\n"
"\n"
"  col:      0   1   2   3\n"
"  row 0:  [ 0   1   2   3 ]     row(1) covers flat 4, 5, 6, 7\n"
"  row 1:  [ 4   5   6   7 ]     col(2) covers flat 2, 6, 10\n"
"  row 2:  [ 8   9  10  11 ]              (a jump of 4 per step)\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../gpu-safety/bounds-checks.md:76
msgid ""
"`MatrixView32` wraps a plain `&[f32]` parameter together with its row width. "
"Asking it for a row or a column performs **one** check -- \"does the whole "
"strip fit inside the slice?\" -- and returns a view whose reads need no "
"further checks:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../gpu-safety/bounds-checks.md:81
msgid ""
"let a_mat = MatrixView32::new(a, k);   // A is m rows, each k wide\n"
"let b_mat = MatrixView32::new(b, n);   // B is k rows, each n wide\n"
"\n"
"if let Some(a_row) = a_mat.row(row, k)      // one check: A's whole row\n"
"    && let Some(b_col) = b_mat.col(col, k)  // one check: B's whole column\n"
"{\n"
"    // every read through a_row / b_col is now check-free\n"
"}\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../gpu-safety/bounds-checks.md:92
msgid ""
"If a check fails you get `None` and no pointer is ever created. There is no "
"`unsafe` anywhere: reading cannot race, so any number of threads may hold "
"overlapping read views."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../gpu-safety/bounds-checks.md:96
msgid "Fusing the dot product: `zip_exact`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../gpu-safety/bounds-checks.md:98
msgid ""
"Iterating one view is already check-free: an iterator's \"is there a next "
"element?\" test *is* the loop's exit condition, which even a raw pointer "
"loop needs. But a dot product iterates two views, and zipping two iterators "
"normally checks both sides every step. `zip_exact` checks **once** that the "
"row and the column have the same length, then drives both with a single "
"counter:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../gpu-safety/bounds-checks.md:105
msgid ""
"if let Some(pair) = a_row.zip_exact(b_col) {\n"
"    let mut sum = 0.0f32;\n"
"    for (x, y) in pair {\n"
"        sum += x * y;\n"
"    }\n"
"}\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../gpu-safety/bounds-checks.md:114
msgid "The loop that comes out the other end is:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../gpu-safety/bounds-checks.md:116
msgid "load a, load b, fma, advance, compare, branch\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../gpu-safety/bounds-checks.md:120
msgid ""
"-- exactly the shape of a hand-written raw-pointer loop. The [`gemm_views`]"
"(https://github.com/NVlabs/cuda-oxide/tree/main/crates/rustc-codegen-cuda/"
"examples/gemm_views) example proves this literally: it compiles each safe "
"kernel next to an `unsafe` raw-pointer twin and verifies that the PTX has "
"the same branch counts, the same memory instructions, no traps in the loop, "
"and bit-identical results on the GPU. Measured at 1024x1024x1024:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../gpu-safety/bounds-checks.md:0
msgid "Kernel"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../gpu-safety/bounds-checks.md:0
msgid "GFLOPS"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../gpu-safety/bounds-checks.md:0
msgid "Notes"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../gpu-safety/bounds-checks.md:0
msgid "`gemm` (plain `a[i]`)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../gpu-safety/bounds-checks.md:0
